@node Overview, Build Outputs, Top, Top
@chapter Overview

XEmacs builds on macOS as a self-contained @file{.app} bundle and can be
packaged into several distribution formats.  The build system is CMake; the
packaging layer is CMake's @command{cpack} together with a few custom CMake
scripts.

@menu
* Current Status::  What changed in the CMake packaging layer.
@end menu

@node Current Status, , Overview, Overview
@section Current Status

The macOS packaging was simplified in two stages:

@enumerate
@item
Seven build bugs were fixed across four CMake files so that the @file{.app}
bundle ships complete lisp, info, and etc directories and boots standalone
from the mounted disk image.  These fixes are covered in the chapters that
follow where the design they produced is described.

@item
The bespoke @code{xemacs-dmg} custom target was removed.  It had hand-rolled
the DMG with @command{cp -R} + @command{hdiutil create}, duplicating work the
install rules already express.  @command{cpack -G DragNDrop} now produces the
disk image as the sole DMG path, carrying both the native
@file{Applications -> /Applications} drag affordance and the guided install
scripts.  The net change was approximately seventy lines deleted.
@end enumerate

@noindent
What remains is a small set of CMake modules:

@table @file
@item cmake/XEmacsCPackBundle.cmake
Defines @code{xemacs_setup_app_bundle()}, which assembles the @file{.app}.

@item cmake/XEmacsInfo.cmake
Builds the Texinfo manuals into @file{.info} pages.

@item cmake/CPackDragNDrop.cmake
A per-generator override applied only when @command{cpack -G DragNDrop} runs.

@item cmake/CPackDragNDropStage.cmake
A staging script that relocates the app and adds the guided install scripts.

@item cmake/XEmacsCPack.cmake
Top-level CPack configuration and generation of the guided scripts.
@end table

@node Build Outputs, App Bundle Layout, Overview, Top
@chapter Build Outputs

The macOS build produces four distinct outputs, each serving a different
audience.  They have @strong{distinct filenames} so they coexist in the build
directory without clobbering each other.

@table @code
@item cmake --install --prefix=/usr
A Filesystem Hierarchy Standard (FHS) tree laid out under the prefix:
@file{prefix/Applications/XEmacs.app}, @file{prefix/share/xemacs-@var{ver}/}
(lisp, etc, info), @file{prefix/lib/xemacs-@var{ver}/} (archlib, modules).
Use this for a system install managed by a package manager.

@item cpack
With no @option{-G} flag, produces @file{xemacs-@var{ver}-@var{arch}.tar.gz},
a tarball of the same FHS tree.  Use this for redistribution as a compressed
archive.

@item cpack -G DragNDrop
Produces @file{XEmacs-@var{ver}-@var{arch}.dmg}, a disk image with
@file{XEmacs.app} at the root and a native @file{Applications -> /Applications}
symlink for drag-and-drop install, @emph{plus} the guided
@file{Install.command} and @file{Uninstall.command} scripts.  Use this for end
users on macOS: they can drag the app into @file{/Applications} or double-click
the installer.

@item make @r{(or @code{make xemacs-app-bundle})}
Builds the @file{.app} in the build tree only.  This is the input the
packaging commands consume; it is not itself a distribution artifact.
@end table

@noindent
The capitalisation is deliberate: @file{XEmacs-...} signals the GUI drag DMG;
@file{xemacs-...} signals the FHS tarball.  They never share a filename.

@node App Bundle Layout, Building the App Bundle, Build Outputs, Top
@chapter App Bundle Layout

The @file{.app} follows the macOS Application Bundle specification.  Everything
the editor needs at runtime lives inside the bundle, so it is self-contained
and can boot from the mounted disk image before installation.

@menu
* Bundle Contents::        The directory structure and what each holds.
* Compatibility Symlinks:: Why @file{Contents/lib} and @file{Contents/share} exist.
@end menu

@node Bundle Contents, Compatibility Symlinks, App Bundle Layout, App Bundle Layout
@section Bundle Contents

@table @file
@item Contents/MacOS/
All executables and the dump file.  The main binary is installed as
@file{xemacs-@var{ver}}; @file{xemacs} and @file{xemacs-script} are symlinks to
it.  Client/server utilities (@file{etags}, @file{gnuclient}, @file{gnuserv},
@file{ctags}) and @file{xemacs.dmp} also live here.

@item Contents/Resources/
All non-code data, organised as the runtime expects it:
@file{Resources/lib/xemacs-@var{ver}/@var{config}/} (archlib: @file{DOC},
@file{hexl}, @file{movemail}, the versioned dump, and @file{modules/*.so}),
and @file{Resources/share/} (@file{xemacs-@var{ver}/lisp/},
@file{xemacs-@var{ver}/info/}, @file{xemacs-@var{ver}/etc/}, and
@file{man/man1/}).

@item Contents/Info.plist
Generated from @file{cmake/Info.plist.in}; carries the bundle identifier
(@code{org.xemacs.xemacs}), version, display name, and minimum system version.

@item Contents/PkgInfo
The literal eight-byte @samp{APPL????} creator code.

@item Contents/Resources/XEmacs.icns
Generated from @file{etc/xemacs-icon.svg} via @command{sips} (to render each
@math{n}-by-@math{n} and @math{2n}-by-@math{2n} PNG size) and
@command{iconutil -c icns} to assemble the iconset.
@end table

@node Compatibility Symlinks, Building the App Bundle, Bundle Contents, App Bundle Layout
@section Compatibility Symlinks

Two symlinks are created at the @file{Contents/} level:

@example
Contents/lib   -> Resources/lib
Contents/share -> Resources/share
@end example

@cindex paths-find-emacs-roots
@cindex runtime path resolution
The Lisp runtime's @code{paths-find-emacs-roots} walks up from @file{MacOS/} to
@file{Contents/} and checks for @file{lib/xemacs-@var{ver}/} and the
@file{lisp/} + @file{etc/} directories.  Because the actual data lives under
@file{Resources/}, the symlinks satisfy both the macOS bundle specification and
the runtime's path-finding.  A third symlink, @file{Contents/lib-src}, is
@emph{intentionally omitted}: the runtime searches for @file{lib-src} at the
root to distinguish installed from in-tree builds; its absence is the correct
``this is an installed build'' signal, causing the runtime to fall back to the
configure-time paths resolved against @file{Contents/}.

@cindex ln -sfn
Both symlinks use @command{ln -sfn}, not @command{ln -sf}.  The @option{-n}
flag treats an existing symlink-to-directory as a normal file rather than
dereferencing it.  Without @option{-n}, a second run dereferences the existing
@file{Contents/lib} symlink, descends into @file{Resources/lib/}, and writes a
@file{lib} entry @emph{inside} it---producing @file{Resources/lib/lib} and a
recursive loop that breaks both the build and @command{cpack -G DragNDrop}.

@node Building the App Bundle, Info Pages, App Bundle Layout, Top
@chapter Building the App Bundle

The bundle is assembled by @code{xemacs_setup_app_bundle()} in
@file{cmake/XEmacsCPackBundle.cmake}.  It is included from the root
@file{CMakeLists.txt} after @file{src/} is added (so the binary exists) and
after the info-page setup (so the bundle can depend on the info outputs).

@menu
* The Stamp Script::       How the bundle contents are staged.
* Build Dependencies::     Why the bundle depends on @code{build-all} and @code{dump}.
* The Install Rule::       How @code{install(DIRECTORY)} stages the bundle.
@end menu

@node The Stamp Script, Build Dependencies, Building the App Bundle, Building the App Bundle
@section The Stamp Script

@cindex build_xemacs_app.sh
@cindex stamp file
Rather than issuing dozens of @command{cmake -E copy} commands at configure
time---which would fail because build-tree artifacts (the binary, lisp, info,
modules) do not exist yet---the function generates a shell script,
@file{build_xemacs_app.sh}, and runs it as the body of a single
@code{add_custom_command}.  The command's @code{OUTPUT} is a stamp file; the
@code{xemacs-app-bundle} @code{ALL} target depends on that stamp.

The script unconditionally copies whatever is present at run time, guarded by
shell @code{if [ -f ... ]} checks so a partial build does not abort the whole
staging.  It copies: the versioned binary and its symlinks, the client/server
tools, @file{xemacs.dmp}, the lisp tree, the info tree (guarded, since it is
empty when manuals are disabled), the @file{etc/} data, the archlib contents
(@file{DOC}, @file{hexl}, @file{movemail}, the versioned dump), man pages, and
any @file{.so} modules found in the build @file{lib/} directory.  It finishes
by creating the two compatibility symlinks and touching the stamp.

@node Build Dependencies, The Install Rule, The Stamp Script, Building the App Bundle
@section Build Dependencies

@cindex build-all, dependency on
@cindex dump, dependency on
@cindex info_pages, dependency on
The bundle target declares three dependencies that are not obvious but are
load-bearing:

@table @code
@item add_dependencies(xemacs-app-bundle build-all)
The @code{dump} target stops after producing @file{xemacs.dmp}; it does not run
the post-dump byte-compilation (@code{update-elc-2}) or build
@file{finder-inf.el}.  Without @code{build-all}, the lisp snapshot taken by
the stamp script would be incomplete---missing roughly forty @file{.elc} files
and @file{finder-inf.el}---and the shipped app would die with a
@code{void: lisp-dir} error.

@item add_dependencies(xemacs-app-bundle dump)
The stamp script copies whatever @file{xemacs.dmp} happens to be in the build
tree at run time.  A previous CMake run may have left a dump with a stale,
random @code{dump_id}.  Requiring a fresh @code{dump} guarantees the dump_id
matches the current binary before the snapshot is taken.

@item DEPENDS @{@{INFO_OUTPUTS@}@} @r{(on the stamp command)}
File-level dependency on every built @file{.info} output, so makeinfo has
finished writing before the info snapshot is taken.  Without this, split info
files (e.g. @file{lispref.info-7} through @file{lispref.info-9}) can be missing
from the bundle due to a race.
@end table

@node The Install Rule, Info Pages, Build Dependencies, Building the App Bundle
@section The Install Rule

@cindex DESTINATION, relative
@cindex USE_SOURCE_PERMISSIONS
The bundle is installed with:

@example
install(DIRECTORY "$@{CMAKE_BINARY_DIR@}/XEmacs.app"
  DESTINATION "Applications"
  COMPONENT Runtime
  USE_SOURCE_PERMISSIONS
)
@end example

@noindent
Three details are deliberate:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Relative @code{DESTINATION}}.  An absolute destination like
@code{$@{CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX@}/Applications} bakes the literal prefix into the
install script, ignores @option{--prefix}/@env{DESTDIR}, and aborts with
@code{EPERM} before the Lisp/Etc/Documentation components can stage.  The
relative @code{"Applications"} respects the prefix.

@item
@strong{No trailing slash on the source}.  A trailing @code{XEmacs.app/}
copies the bundle's @emph{contents} (i.e. @file{Contents/}) rather than the
@file{XEmacs.app} wrapper, dropping the top-level directory the bundle
specification requires.

@item
@strong{@code{USE_SOURCE_PERMISSIONS}}.  Preserves the executable bits set on
the @file{.command} scripts and @file{bin/} wrappers by the stamp script.
Without it, installed files default to mode @samp{644} and the installer
scripts cannot run.
@end itemize

@node Info Pages, The Drag-and-Drop DMG, Building the App Bundle, Top
@chapter Info Pages

@cindex makeinfo
@cindex XEmacsInfo.cmake
The Texinfo manuals are built by @file{cmake/XEmacsInfo.cmake}.  It locates
@command{makeinfo} (or @command{texi2any}); if neither is found, info pages
are silently skipped.  On macOS, install the @command{texinfo} package (e.g.
@code{brew install texinfo}) to build them.

@menu
* Manual Classification::  SIMPLE vs SUBDIR manuals.
* The dir Index::          The @file{info/dir} index file.
@end menu

@node Manual Classification, The dir Index, Info Pages, Info Pages
@section Manual Classification

Manuals are split into two lists:

@table @code
@item SIMPLE_MANUALS
@code{beta cl emodules external-widget info standards termcap widget xemacs-faq mac-packaging}---single
@file{.texi} files at the top of @file{man/}, built with @option{--no-split}
into one @file{.info} each.

@item SUBDIR_MANUALS
@code{xemacs lispref internals new-users-guide}---the main @file{.texi} lives
in a subdirectory and is built with @option{-I} so its @code{@{include@}}
directives resolve.  These can produce split info files
(@file{.info} plus @file{.info-1} through @file{.info-N}).
@end table

@node The dir Index, The Drag-and-Drop DMG, Manual Classification, Info Pages
@section The dir Index

@cindex info/dir
@cindex Info directory index
The top-level @file{info/dir} file is a @strong{source file}, not generated.
Autoconf installs @file{$@{srcdir@}/info/dir} as @file{infodir/dir}; the CMake
build replicates this with an explicit @code{copy} command.  Each manual adds
a @code{* Name: (name).} line to @file{info/dir} so @kbd{C-h i} in XEmacs lists
it.  The install rule @code{FILES_MATCHING} includes the @code{PATTERN "dir"}
so the index ships alongside the manuals.

@node The Drag-and-Drop DMG, Guided Install Scripts, Info Pages, Top
@chapter The Drag-and-Drop DMG

@cindex DragNDrop
@cindex collision, File exists
@command{cpack -G DragNDrop} is the standard CMake generator for producing a
macOS disk image.  Getting it to work with XEmacs required resolving a
collision and exploiting three CMake mechanisms; this chapter is the
centerpiece of the manual.

@menu
* The Collision::              Why @command{cpack -G DragNDrop} failed.
* The Resolution::             The three mechanisms that make it work.
* The Staging Script::        What @file{CPackDragNDropStage.cmake} does.
@end menu

@node The Collision, The Resolution, The Drag-and-Drop DMG, The Drag-and-Drop DMG
@section The Collision

A naive @command{cpack -G DragNDrop} failed with @samp{failed to create
symbolic link '.../Applications': File exists}.  There are two distinct causes:

@table @asis
@item Structural
DragNDrop runs the project's @code{install()} rules into a staging directory,
then creates an @file{Applications -> /Applications} symlink at the staging
root---the drag-and-drop affordance.  But the app install rule (see
@ref{The Install Rule}) stages the app as a @emph{real}
@file{Applications/XEmacs.app/} directory.  Same name, different type: a
directory cannot be replaced by a symlink, so @command{ln -s} aborts.

@item Filename
@code{CPACK_PACKAGE_FILE_NAME} defaults to
@code{xemacs-@var{ver}-@var{arch}}.  DragNDrop writes
@file{xemacs-@var{ver}-@var{arch}.dmg}---the same path the (now-removed)
custom @code{xemacs-dmg} target wrote, and the same stem as the TGZ tarball.
The outputs overwrite each other.
@end table

@noindent
An attempt to fix only the structural cause by moving the app to
@code{DESTINATION "."} let DragNDrop create its symlink, but produced a DMG
root cluttered with FHS directories (@file{share/}, @file{lib/}) alongside the
app---because the Lisp/Etc/Info install rules still stage into the same tree.
A clean drag DMG needs the app at the root @strong{and} the FHS components
excluded.

@node The Resolution, The Staging Script, The Collision, The Drag-and-Drop DMG
@section The Resolution

Three CMake mechanisms, verified against CMake's own modules, make a clean
DragNDrop DMG possible with no install-rule forking and no custom target:

@table @code
@item CPACK_PROJECT_CONFIG_FILE
@cindex CPACK_PROJECT_CONFIG_FILE
CMake includes this file once per generator, resetting
@code{CPACK_GENERATOR} to the current one first (@file{CPack.cmake}, the
per-generator iteration).  So a single override file can change the filename
and component list @strong{for DragNDrop only}, leaving TGZ and
@command{cmake --install} untouched.  The file self-guards with
@code{if(NOT CPACK_GENERATOR STREQUAL "DragNDrop") return() endif()} so it is a
no-op for every other generator.

@item CPACK_PRE_BUILD_SCRIPTS
@cindex CPACK_PRE_BUILD_SCRIPTS
@cindex CPACK_TEMPORARY_DIRECTORY
@cindex CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX, at cpack time
Runs @emph{after} the install rules populate the staging directory and
@emph{before} DragNDrop adds its @file{Applications} symlink.  This is the hook
that relocates the app to the staging root and clears the name.  One subtlety
dominated the implementation: at cpack time, @code{CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX} is the
project's original prefix (e.g. @file{/usr/local}), @strong{not} the staging
directory---only @code{CPACK_INSTALL_SCRIPTS} documents that prefix being set
to staging, and that hook runs @emph{before} the component install.  The
staging root is available as @code{CPACK_TEMPORARY_DIRECTORY} (set by the
@command{cpack} binary itself); the staging script reads that variable, not
@code{CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX}.

@item CPACK_MONOLITHIC_INSTALL
@cindex CPACK_MONOLITHIC_INSTALL
@cindex CPACK_COMPONENTS_GROUPING
Set to @code{ON}.  A component-based install nests each component under a
@file{@var{file-name}-@var{Component}/} subfolder; the app then lands at
@file{staging/XEmacs-...-Runtime/Applications/XEmacs.app} and DragNDrop's
symlink targets the wrong place.  Monolithic install stages everything flat
into @code{CPACK_TEMPORARY_DIRECTORY} directly, so the app lands at
@file{staging/Applications/XEmacs.app} and the symlink targets
@file{staging/Applications} as intended.  The staging script then removes the
FHS residue (@file{share/}, @file{lib/}) the install rules staged, keeping the
drag DMG minimal---the @file{.app} is self-contained, so those components are
redundant on a drag image.
@end table

@noindent
A distinct filename (@code{XEmacs-@var{ver}-@var{arch}}) resolves the
filename collision.

@node The Staging Script, Guided Install Scripts, The Resolution, The Drag-and-Drop DMG
@section The Staging Script

@cindex CPackDragNDropStage.cmake
@file{cmake/CPackDragNDropStage.cmake} runs as the pre-build script.  It does
three jobs, in order:

@enumerate
@item
@cindex file(RENAME)
@cindex idempotency
Relocate the app from @file{@var{staging}/Applications/XEmacs.app} to
@file{@var{staging}/XEmacs.app}, so it sits at the DMG root (the drag-install
convention) and frees the @samp{Applications} name.  Guarded with
@code{if(EXISTS ... AND NOT EXISTS ...)} so it is idempotent across reruns
within one @command{cpack} invocation.

@item
@cindex file(REMOVE_RECURSE)
Remove @file{@var{staging}/Applications}, @file{@var{staging}/share}, and
@file{@var{staging}/lib}.  The first clears the name for DragNDrop's symlink
(this is the original collision, resolved); the latter two drop the FHS
residue, keeping the drag DMG minimal.

@item
@cindex file(INSTALL)
@cindex USE_SOURCE_PERMISSIONS
Stage the guided install scripts at the DMG root alongside the app:
@file{bin/} (the CLI wrappers), @file{install-cli.sh}, @file{Install.command},
@file{Uninstall.command}, and @file{README.txt}.  These are generated at
configure time (see @ref{Guided Install Scripts}) and staged via
@code{file(INSTALL ... USE_SOURCE_PERMISSIONS)}, which preserves the
executable bits set during configure.
@end enumerate

@node Guided Install Scripts, The FHS Tarball, The Drag-and-Drop DMG, Top
@chapter Guided Install Scripts

@cindex Install.command
@cindex Uninstall.command
@cindex bin wrappers
The drag DMG ships a set of guided install scripts alongside the app, so users
who want more than a bare drag-install can double-click a single file.  These
are generated at configure time in the build tree (@file{build/dmg-bin/},
@file{build/dmg-install-cli.sh}, @file{build/dmg-install.command},
@file{build/dmg-uninstall.command}) and staged by the pre-build script.

@table @file
@item Install.command
A one-click installer.  Double-click to @command{ditto} the @file{.app} into
@file{/Applications} (ditto preserves the internal symlinks, unlike
@command{cp -R}), set up the CLI symlinks in @file{~/.local/bin}, copy
@file{Uninstall.command} into @file{~/.local/bin/remove-xemacs} for permanent
access after the disk image is ejected, @command{open} the app, and post a
notification.  @file{~/.local/bin} is on the default @code{PATH} on modern
macOS, so no @command{sudo} is required.

@item Uninstall.command
A smart uninstaller.  Removes the CLI symlinks from @file{~/.local/bin}
regardless of whether the disk image is still mounted, and is safe to run even
if @file{XEmacs.app} has already been moved to the Trash.  Reports the app's
status and posts a notification.

@item bin/@r{(directory)}
Per-tool wrapper scripts (@file{xemacs}, @file{xemacs-script}, @file{etags},
@file{gnuclient}, @file{gnuserv}) that @code{exec} the real binary inside
@file{/Applications/XEmacs.app/Contents/MacOS/}.  Useful before installation,
while the disk image is mounted.

@item install-cli.sh
Creates the @file{~/.local/bin} symlinks without the @file{.app} copy, for
users who prefer the terminal.  Resolves the @file{.app} from
@file{/Applications} first, falling back to the disk-image mount location
(this script's own directory).
@end table

@noindent
Both install paths land in @file{/Applications}: @file{Install.command} copies
there directly; the @file{Applications} symlink is the Finder drag target.
They do not conflict.

@node The FHS Tarball, Design Decisions, Guided Install Scripts, Top
@chapter The FHS Tarball

@cindex TGZ
@cindex cmake --install
The default @command{cpack} (no @option{-G}) and @command{cmake --install}
produce the FHS layout, untouched by the DragNDrop work.  This is the path for
package managers and system installs:

@example
cmake --install build/console --prefix=/usr
@end example

@noindent
lays out @file{/usr/Applications/XEmacs.app}, @file{/usr/share/xemacs-@var{ver}/}
(lisp, etc, info), and @file{/usr/lib/xemacs-@var{ver}/@var{config}/} (archlib,
modules).  The installed app boots and resolves its lisp and info directories
inside the bundle, exactly as the drag-DMG app does.

@noindent
@command{cpack} (bare) produces @file{xemacs-@var{ver}-@var{arch}.tar.gz}, a
compressed archive of the same tree.  Because
@code{CPACK_GENERATOR} is @code{"TGZ"} on macOS (not @code{DragNDrop}), bare
@command{cpack} and @command{make package} always produce only the tarball; the
DMG is produced explicitly via @command{cpack -G DragNDrop}.

@node Design Decisions, Troubleshooting, The FHS Tarball, Top
@chapter Design Decisions

This chapter collects the rationale behind each non-obvious choice, as a
reference for future maintainers.

@table @asis
@item @command{ln -sfn} for compatibility symlinks
The @option{-n} flag prevents dereferencing an existing symlink-to-directory
on a rebuild.  Without it, @command{ln -sf} descends into the target directory
and creates a nested loop (@file{Resources/lib/lib}).  This idempotency fix is
applied to both @file{Contents/lib} and @file{Contents/share}.

@item Relative @code{DESTINATION "Applications"} with no trailing slash
An absolute destination bakes the prefix literal into the install script and
aborts with @code{EPERM} before later components stage; a trailing slash on
the source copies the bundle's contents and drops the @file{XEmacs.app}
wrapper.  Both were observed bugs.

@item @code{USE_SOURCE_PERMISSIONS} on the bundle install
Preserves the executable bits on the staged @file{.command} and
@file{bin/} scripts; without it the installed files are mode @samp{644} and
the installer cannot run.

@item @code{add_dependencies(xemacs-app-bundle build-all)}, not just @code{dump}
@code{dump} stops before post-dump byte-compilation and @file{finder-inf.el};
@code{build-all} completes the full lisp pipeline.  Depending on @code{dump}
alone ships a broken app (@code{void: lisp-dir}).

@item The omitted @file{Contents/lib-src} symlink
Its absence is the ``installed build'' signal the runtime checks for; the
runtime then falls back to configure-time paths resolved against
@file{Contents/}.  Adding it would break that detection.

@item @code{CPACK_XEMACS_*} snapshot variables
At cpack time, only @code{CPACK_*} variables are visible (read from
@file{CPackConfig.cmake}); ordinary project variables
(@code{EMACS_VERSION}, @code{XEMACS_APP_BUNDLE_NAME},
@code{CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}) are @strong{not}.  The project snapshots them into
@code{CPACK_XEMACS_*} before @code{include(CPack)} so the override and staging
scripts can read them.

@item Distinct DMG vs tarball filenames
@code{XEmacs-@var{ver}-@var{arch}.dmg} (capital @samp{X}) for the drag DMG,
@code{xemacs-@var{ver}-@var{arch}.tar.gz} for the FHS tarball.  They coexist
in the build directory without clobbering each other.

@item @code{CPACK_TEMPORARY_DIRECTORY}, not @code{CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX}, in the staging script
At cpack time @code{CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX} is the original project prefix, not
the staging root.  Using it would attempt to write to (e.g.)
@file{/usr/local/bin} and abort with @code{EPERM}.  The staging root is
@code{CPACK_TEMPORARY_DIRECTORY}.

@item @code{CPACK_MONOLITHIC_INSTALL=ON} for DragNDrop
A component-based install nests each component in a subfolder, mis-targeting
DragNDrop's symlink.  Monolithic install stages flat into
@code{CPACK_TEMPORARY_DIRECTORY}, so the symlink lands correctly.

@item Pre-build script over install script
@code{CPACK_INSTALL_SCRIPTS} runs @emph{before} the component install (it is a
``preinstall'' step), so the app is not yet staged when it runs.  The
relocation must happen @emph{after} staging, so
@code{CPACK_PRE_BUILD_SCRIPTS} is the correct hook.
@end table

@node Troubleshooting, , Design Decisions, Top
@chapter Troubleshooting

@table @asis
@item @samp{makeinfo not found - info pages will not be built}
Install the @command{texinfo} package.  On macOS with Homebrew:
@code{brew install texinfo}.  The brew keg is not on @code{PATH} by default;
link it or add @file{$(brew --prefix texinfo)/bin} to your @code{PATH}.

@item @samp{failed to create symbolic link '.../Applications': File exists}
This was the DragNDrop collision, now resolved by the pre-build staging
script.  If it reappears, check that @code{CPACK_PRE_BUILD_SCRIPTS} is set in
@file{CPackDragNDrop.cmake} and that @code{CPACK_MONOLITHIC_INSTALL} is
@code{ON}; without monolithic install the app nests in a component subfolder
and the collision returns.

@item DMG prompts for a license agreement on mount
@cindex SLA
@cindex CPACK_RESOURCE_FILE_LICENSE
This is pre-existing behaviour: @code{CPACK_RESOURCE_FILE_LICENSE} is set to
the project @file{COPYING} (the GPL), and CMake's SLA mechanism surfaces it on
mount.  It is not introduced by the packaging changes.  To suppress it for
testing, mount with @command{echo y | hdiutil attach ...} or pass
@option{-nobrowse}.

@item The app dies with @code{void: lisp-dir} or missing @file{.elc} files
The bundle was built against an incomplete lisp tree.  Rebuild with
@code{make build-all} before packaging, or ensure
@code{add_dependencies(xemacs-app-bundle build-all)} is present.  A stale
@code{dump_id} can also cause this; @code{make dump} before packaging
refreshes it.

@item Symlink loops (@file{Resources/lib/lib}) after a rebuild
The compatibility symlinks were created with @command{ln -sf} instead of
@command{ln -sfn}.  The @option{-n} flag is required, not optional.  Remove
the looped directory and rebuild.

@item Installed scripts have mode @samp{644} and will not run
The bundle install rule is missing @code{USE_SOURCE_PERMISSIONS}.  Without it,
installed files default to @samp{644}; the @file{.command} and @file{bin/}
scripts lose their executable bit.
@end table

